 ## Background
-The NVIDIA GeForce3 Volume Rendering Pipeline was introduced in the context of interactive volume rendering using multi-dimensional transfer functions. It was developed to address the performance limitations of earlier hardware implementations, specifically the SGI Octane2 method which relied on scratch buffers and expensive copy operations [S1, S2]. The method leverages the NVIDIA Register Combiner extension and the GeForce3's support for per-fragment dependent texture reads and 4-way multi-texturing [S1, S2]. It was presented in papers by Kindlmann et al. around 2001 [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+The NVIDIA GeForce3 Volume Rendering Pipeline was introduced in the context of interactive volume rendering using multi-dimensional transfer functions [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It was developed to address the performance limitations of earlier hardware implementations, specifically the SGI Octane2 method which relied on scratch buffers and expensive copy operations [S1, S2]. The method leverages the NVIDIA Register Combiner extension and the GeForce3's support for per-fragment dependent texture reads and 4-way multi-texturing [S1, S2]. It was presented in papers by Kindlmann et al. around 2001 [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
 
